{"module":"destroyed","voice":"female","duration":654.6,"segments":[{"el":"title","text":"Burned, flooded and taken: the communities America destroyed","start":0.0,"end":4.24,"words":[[0.06,0.43],[0.69,1.13],[1.13,1.36],[1.36,1.87],[2.13,2.28],[2.28,3.02],[3.02,3.54],[3.54,4.13]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Again and again, when Black communities built wealth, voted or simply grew, they were attacked: burned by mobs, driven off their land, flooded for dams, seized for parks and beaches, or bulldozed for highways.","start":5.04,"end":19.79,"words":[[5.09,5.41],[5.41,5.66],[5.66,6.06],[6.35,6.59],[6.59,6.91],[6.91,7.73],[7.73,8.05],[8.05,8.46],[8.74,9.23],[9.23,9.31],[9.31,9.8],[9.8,10.12],[10.41,10.65],[10.65,10.81],[10.81,11.3],[11.58,11.91],[11.91,12.15],[12.15,12.56],[12.84,13.33],[13.33,13.49],[13.49,13.74],[13.74,14.14],[14.43,14.91],[14.91,15.08],[15.08,15.48],[15.77,16.09],[16.09,16.25],[16.25,16.58],[16.58,16.82],[16.82,17.39],[17.68,17.76],[17.76,18.41],[18.41,18.57],[18.57,19.22]]},{"el":"intro","text":"Each loss wiped out homes and businesses that would have passed wealth down to the next generation.","start":20.14,"end":25.84,"words":[[20.19,20.41],[20.41,20.62],[20.62,20.98],[20.98,21.2],[21.2,21.56],[21.56,21.77],[21.77,22.35],[22.35,22.56],[22.56,22.78],[22.78,22.99],[22.99,23.28],[23.28,23.57],[23.57,23.85],[23.85,24.0],[24.0,24.14],[24.14,24.5],[24.5,25.36]]},{"el":"intro","text":"This module tells those stories, and they are all on the map.","start":26.19,"end":29.98,"words":[[26.24,26.47],[26.47,26.91],[26.91,27.21],[27.21,27.5],[27.5,28.02],[28.28,28.5],[28.5,28.72],[28.72,28.8],[28.8,28.94],[28.94,29.09],[29.09,29.24],[29.24,29.54]]},{"el":"h0","text":"Greenwood, Tulsa: “Black Wall Street”","start":30.33,"end":33.23,"words":[[30.38,31.03],[31.32,31.81],[32.09,32.42],[32.42,32.66],[32.66,33.07]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In the early 1900s, Greenwood was home to about 10,000 Black residents.","start":33.93,"end":39.59,"words":[[33.98,34.12],[34.12,34.27],[34.27,34.48],[34.48,35.62],[35.87,36.37],[36.37,36.59],[36.59,36.87],[36.87,37.01],[37.01,37.37],[37.37,38.08],[38.08,38.37],[38.37,39.08]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Its main street had hotels, theaters, groceries, doctors' and lawyers' offices, churches, two newspapers and a hospital.","start":39.94,"end":47.63,"words":[[40.0,40.19],[40.19,40.44],[40.44,40.77],[40.77,40.96],[40.96,41.47],[41.69,42.14],[42.37,43.01],[43.23,43.62],[43.62,43.81],[43.81,44.13],[44.13,44.58],[44.8,45.32],[45.54,45.67],[45.67,46.31],[46.31,46.5],[46.5,46.63],[46.63,47.21]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Oklahoma's segregation laws kept Black dollars inside the district, and they circulated there.","start":47.98,"end":53.37,"words":[[48.03,48.71],[48.71,49.38],[49.38,49.57],[49.57,49.81],[49.81,50.06],[50.06,50.37],[50.37,50.73],[50.73,50.86],[50.86,51.41],[51.62,51.81],[51.81,51.99],[51.99,52.67],[52.67,52.91]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Booker T. Washington is credited with calling it the “Negro Wall Street.”","start":53.72,"end":58.17,"words":[[53.77,54.05],[54.05,54.25],[54.6,55.15],[55.15,55.29],[55.29,55.84],[55.84,56.04],[56.04,56.39],[56.39,56.53],[56.53,56.66],[56.66,57.08],[57.08,57.28],[57.28,57.7]]},{"el":"s0","text":"O.W. Gurley, J.B. Stradford and other Black entrepreneurs built it on land they owned.","start":58.52,"end":64.8,"words":[[58.58,59.24],[59.57,59.91],[60.14,60.61],[60.94,61.47],[61.47,61.67],[61.67,61.87],[61.87,62.14],[62.14,63.0],[63.0,63.27],[63.27,63.4],[63.4,63.53],[63.53,63.8],[63.8,64.0],[64.0,64.33]]},{"el":"s0","text":"May 31–June 1, 1921.","start":65.15,"end":68.09,"words":[[65.2,65.39],[65.39,66.1],[66.1,66.36],[66.58,67.68]]},{"el":"s0","text":"A Black teenager, Dick Rowland, was arrested after a white elevator operator accused him of assault.","start":68.44,"end":74.39,"words":[[68.49,68.61],[68.61,68.87],[68.87,69.44],[69.66,69.85],[69.85,70.36],[70.59,70.78],[70.78,71.22],[71.22,71.48],[71.48,71.61],[71.61,71.86],[71.86,72.37],[72.37,72.88],[72.88,73.26],[73.26,73.45],[73.45,73.58],[73.58,73.96]]},{"el":"s0","text":"A newspaper story inflamed the city.","start":74.74,"end":77.27,"words":[[74.8,74.93],[74.93,75.52],[75.52,75.85],[75.85,76.38],[76.38,76.51],[76.51,76.84]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Black World War I veterans went to the courthouse to stop a lynching, and a white mob, many of them deputized by officials, invaded Greenwood.","start":77.62,"end":86.27,"words":[[77.7,77.97],[77.97,78.24],[78.24,78.38],[78.38,78.51],[78.51,79.05],[79.05,79.32],[79.32,79.46],[79.46,79.59],[79.59,80.07],[80.07,80.2],[80.2,80.47],[80.47,80.61],[80.61,81.15],[81.38,81.59],[81.59,81.72],[81.72,81.99],[81.99,82.26],[82.5,82.77],[82.77,82.91],[82.91,83.11],[83.11,83.78],[83.78,83.99],[83.99,84.53],[84.76,85.3],[85.3,85.84]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Over about 18 hours they burned 35 city blocks, including about 1,256 homes.","start":86.62,"end":94.44,"words":[[86.68,86.98],[86.98,87.36],[87.36,87.74],[87.74,88.04],[88.04,88.26],[88.26,88.57],[88.57,89.17],[89.17,89.47],[89.47,89.92],[90.19,90.79],[90.79,91.16],[91.16,93.43],[93.43,93.88]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The 2001 Oklahoma commission found that airplanes were used in the attack and estimated that 100 to 300 people were killed.","start":94.79,"end":103.54,"words":[[94.83,94.98],[94.98,95.87],[95.87,96.61],[96.61,97.13],[97.13,97.5],[97.5,97.72],[97.72,98.31],[98.31,98.46],[98.46,98.76],[98.76,98.9],[98.9,99.05],[99.05,99.35],[99.35,99.57],[99.57,100.31],[100.31,100.53],[100.53,101.27],[101.27,101.42],[101.42,102.16],[102.16,102.53],[102.53,102.68],[102.68,103.05]]},{"el":"s0","text":"About 6,000 Black residents were held in internment camps.","start":103.89,"end":107.62,"words":[[103.94,104.25],[104.25,104.94],[104.94,105.2],[105.2,105.76],[105.76,105.89],[105.89,106.14],[106.14,106.26],[106.26,106.83],[106.83,107.21]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Afterward.","start":107.97,"end":108.87,"words":[[108.02,108.47]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Insurance companies denied claims under “riot” clauses, and the city tried to rezone the burned district so it couldn't be rebuilt.","start":109.22,"end":117.34,"words":[[109.27,109.92],[109.92,110.5],[110.5,110.94],[110.94,111.37],[111.37,111.66],[111.66,112.02],[112.02,112.53],[112.78,113.0],[113.0,113.15],[113.15,113.44],[113.44,113.8],[113.8,113.94],[113.94,114.38],[114.38,114.52],[114.52,114.81],[114.81,115.39],[115.39,115.61],[115.61,115.75],[115.75,116.19],[116.19,116.33],[116.33,116.84]]},{"el":"s0","text":"No one was ever prosecuted.","start":117.69,"end":119.7,"words":[[117.74,117.93],[117.93,118.12],[118.12,118.31],[118.31,118.5],[118.5,119.26]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Residents rebuilt anyway.","start":120.05,"end":121.8,"words":[[120.1,120.63],[120.63,120.99],[120.99,121.4]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Then, in the 1960s and 1970s, urban renewal and Interstate 244 cut through Greenwood again.","start":122.15,"end":130.75,"words":[[122.2,122.5],[122.76,122.91],[122.91,123.07],[123.07,124.12],[124.12,124.35],[124.35,125.55],[125.82,126.12],[126.12,126.65],[126.65,126.87],[126.87,127.55],[127.55,128.83],[128.83,129.06],[129.06,129.29],[129.29,129.82],[129.82,130.19]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Still unresolved.","start":131.1,"end":132.94,"words":[[131.14,131.5],[131.5,132.41]]},{"el":"s0","text":"The 2001 state commission recommended reparations; none have been paid by the state.","start":133.29,"end":139.21,"words":[[133.34,133.48],[133.48,134.33],[134.33,134.68],[134.68,135.18],[135.18,135.89],[135.89,136.74],[136.99,137.21],[137.21,137.42],[137.42,137.63],[137.63,137.92],[137.92,138.13],[138.13,138.27],[138.27,138.7]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Searches for mass graves at Oaklawn Cemetery began in 2020, and in 2024 one set of remains was identified as C.L. Daniel, a World War I veteran.","start":139.56,"end":150.85,"words":[[139.6,140.04],[140.04,140.19],[140.19,140.41],[140.41,140.85],[140.85,141.0],[141.0,141.44],[141.44,142.03],[142.03,142.4],[142.4,142.55],[142.55,143.51],[143.77,143.99],[143.99,144.14],[144.14,145.17],[145.17,145.4],[145.4,145.62],[145.62,145.77],[145.77,146.28],[146.28,146.5],[146.5,147.32],[147.32,147.46],[147.46,147.91],[148.28,148.79],[149.05,149.2],[149.2,149.5],[149.5,149.64],[149.64,149.79],[149.79,150.38]]},{"el":"s0","text":"Also in 2024, the Oklahoma Supreme Court dismissed the last survivors' lawsuit.","start":151.2,"end":157.22,"words":[[151.27,151.64],[151.64,151.79],[151.79,152.9],[153.16,153.31],[153.31,154.05],[154.05,154.5],[154.5,154.72],[154.72,155.24],[155.24,155.39],[155.39,155.68],[155.68,156.27],[156.27,156.72]]},{"el":"s0","text":"In January 2025 the U.S. Justice Department called the massacre a civil rights crime of unparalleled magnitude, but said no one left alive could be prosecuted.","start":157.57,"end":168.86,"words":[[157.62,157.76],[157.76,158.42],[158.42,159.59],[159.59,159.74],[159.74,160.11],[160.47,160.98],[160.98,161.64],[161.64,161.94],[161.94,162.08],[162.08,162.52],[162.52,162.67],[162.67,163.03],[163.03,163.4],[163.4,163.77],[163.77,163.91],[163.91,164.65],[164.65,165.38],[165.63,165.85],[165.85,166.07],[166.07,166.29],[166.29,166.51],[166.51,166.81],[166.81,167.17],[167.17,167.39],[167.39,167.54],[167.54,168.42]]},{"el":"h1","text":"Burned: a pattern, not an accident","start":169.76,"end":172.52,"words":[[169.82,170.21],[170.48,170.63],[170.63,171.09],[171.36,171.59],[171.59,171.74],[171.74,172.36]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Tulsa was one of many.","start":173.22,"end":174.61,"words":[[173.22,173.5],[173.5,173.67],[173.67,173.84],[173.84,173.95],[173.95,174.24]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The death tolls below are estimates, and many are disputed because officials undercounted.","start":174.96,"end":180.23,"words":[[175.02,175.15],[175.15,175.36],[175.36,175.7],[175.7,176.04],[176.04,176.1],[176.1,176.72],[176.95,177.16],[177.16,177.43],[177.43,177.5],[177.5,178.11],[178.11,178.45],[178.45,178.92],[178.92,179.74]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The pattern is clear: a mob, often set off by an accusation against a Black man or by Black voting, organizing or success, followed by fire.","start":180.58,"end":189.32,"words":[[180.62,180.76],[180.76,181.1],[181.1,181.24],[181.24,181.59],[181.83,181.97],[181.97,182.25],[182.49,182.77],[182.77,182.97],[182.97,183.11],[183.11,183.32],[183.32,183.46],[183.46,184.15],[184.15,184.57],[184.57,184.71],[184.71,184.98],[184.98,185.19],[185.19,185.26],[185.26,185.47],[185.47,185.75],[185.75,186.23],[186.47,187.1],[187.1,187.17],[187.17,187.65],[187.89,188.31],[188.31,188.52],[188.52,188.86]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Colfax, Louisiana, 1873: an estimated 62–153 Black men killed defending a courthouse after an election.","start":189.67,"end":199.31,"words":[[189.71,190.28],[190.52,191.23],[191.47,192.6],[192.85,192.99],[192.99,193.69],[193.69,195.81],[195.81,196.09],[196.09,196.3],[196.3,196.58],[196.58,197.15],[197.15,197.29],[197.29,197.78],[197.78,198.06],[198.06,198.2],[198.2,198.77]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Supreme Court then let the killers go (U.S. v. Cruikshank, 1876).","start":199.66,"end":205.09,"words":[[199.71,199.84],[199.84,200.22],[200.22,200.42],[200.42,200.61],[200.61,200.81],[200.81,200.93],[200.93,201.26],[201.26,201.45],[201.45,201.77],[202.1,202.29],[202.61,203.26],[203.49,204.58]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Wilmington, North Carolina, 1898: the only successful coup on U.S. soil.","start":205.44,"end":211.92,"words":[[205.49,206.19],[206.43,206.64],[206.64,207.34],[207.58,208.7],[208.94,209.08],[209.08,209.43],[209.43,210.06],[210.06,210.26],[210.26,210.4],[210.4,210.75],[211.1,211.45]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The Black newspaper was burned and at least 60 people were killed.","start":212.27,"end":216.37,"words":[[212.32,212.47],[212.47,212.76],[212.76,213.42],[213.42,213.64],[213.64,213.93],[213.93,214.15],[214.15,214.3],[214.3,214.59],[214.59,215.03],[215.03,215.39],[215.39,215.54],[215.54,215.91]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Atlanta 1906, Springfield 1908, Slocum 1910: each sparked by rumors or accusations.","start":216.72,"end":226.09,"words":[[216.77,217.38],[217.38,218.6],[218.91,219.69],[219.69,220.83],[221.13,221.66],[221.66,222.61],[222.92,223.18],[223.18,223.62],[223.62,223.88],[223.88,224.32],[224.32,224.4],[224.4,225.45]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Springfield led directly to the founding of the NAACP.","start":226.44,"end":230.09,"words":[[226.49,227.06],[227.06,227.25],[227.25,227.82],[227.82,227.94],[227.94,228.07],[228.07,228.51],[228.51,228.64],[228.64,228.77],[228.77,229.65]]},{"el":"s1","text":"East St. Louis, 1917: about 6,000 people left homeless after white workers attacked Black workers.","start":230.44,"end":238.5,"words":[[230.51,230.74],[230.74,231.11],[231.48,231.78],[232.04,233.23],[233.49,233.86],[233.86,234.68],[234.68,235.05],[235.05,235.35],[235.35,235.87],[235.87,236.17],[236.17,236.47],[236.47,236.84],[236.84,237.21],[237.21,237.51],[237.51,237.95]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Red Summer, 1919: violence in more than two dozen cities.","start":238.85,"end":243.88,"words":[[238.9,239.13],[239.13,239.51],[239.78,240.92],[241.19,241.8],[241.8,241.95],[241.95,242.1],[242.1,242.33],[242.33,242.48],[242.48,242.86],[242.86,243.32]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In Elaine, Arkansas, Black sharecroppers trying to form a union were massacred, with estimates from about 100 to several hundred dead.","start":244.23,"end":253.13,"words":[[244.28,244.42],[244.42,244.86],[245.11,245.61],[245.87,246.15],[246.15,246.8],[246.8,247.24],[247.24,247.38],[247.38,247.6],[247.6,247.74],[247.74,248.17],[248.17,248.32],[248.32,248.89],[249.15,249.36],[249.36,249.94],[249.94,250.23],[250.23,250.59],[250.59,251.31],[251.31,251.45],[251.45,251.89],[251.89,252.39],[252.39,252.68]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Ocoee, Florida, 1920: Black residents tried to vote.","start":253.48,"end":257.94,"words":[[253.53,253.85],[254.08,254.58],[254.81,255.7],[255.92,256.18],[256.18,256.75],[256.75,257.07],[257.07,257.2],[257.2,257.52]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The entire Black community was burned out.","start":258.29,"end":261.06,"words":[[258.34,258.48],[258.48,258.9],[258.9,259.18],[259.18,259.81],[259.81,260.02],[260.02,260.3],[260.3,260.58]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Rosewood, Florida, 1923: a white woman's accusation led to a whole town being burned.","start":261.41,"end":267.27,"words":[[261.47,261.94],[262.15,262.63],[262.83,263.85],[264.06,264.17],[264.17,264.41],[264.41,264.77],[264.77,265.37],[265.37,265.54],[265.54,265.66],[265.66,265.78],[265.78,266.02],[266.02,266.26],[266.26,266.56],[266.56,266.85]]},{"el":"s1","text":"In 1994 Florida became the first state to pay reparations for racial violence.","start":267.62,"end":273.89,"words":[[267.67,267.82],[267.82,268.97],[268.97,269.5],[269.5,269.96],[269.96,270.11],[270.11,270.42],[270.42,270.8],[270.8,270.95],[270.95,271.18],[271.18,272.02],[272.02,272.18],[272.18,272.63],[272.63,273.32]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Philadelphia, 1985: police dropped a bomb on the MOVE house; 11 people died, including 5 children, and 61 homes in a Black neighborhood burned.","start":274.24,"end":285.19,"words":[[274.26,275.04],[275.29,276.44],[276.69,277.04],[277.04,277.4],[277.4,277.54],[277.54,277.76],[277.76,277.9],[277.9,278.04],[278.04,278.26],[278.26,278.62],[278.87,279.3],[279.3,279.65],[279.65,280.01],[280.26,280.83],[280.83,281.12],[281.12,281.76],[282.01,282.22],[282.22,282.87],[282.87,283.23],[283.23,283.37],[283.37,283.51],[283.51,283.8],[283.8,284.37],[284.37,284.73]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Chinese immigrants were attacked the same way: in Los Angeles in 1871, in Rock Springs, Wyoming, in 1885, and in Tacoma, Washington, which expelled its entire Chinese population that year.","start":285.54,"end":300.71,"words":[[285.59,286.12],[286.12,286.8],[286.8,286.95],[286.95,287.33],[287.33,287.48],[287.48,287.78],[287.78,288.09],[288.35,288.51],[288.51,288.73],[288.73,289.34],[289.34,289.49],[289.49,290.7],[290.97,291.12],[291.12,291.35],[291.35,291.88],[292.14,292.83],[293.09,293.24],[293.24,294.31],[294.57,294.8],[294.8,294.95],[294.95,295.56],[295.82,296.51],[296.77,297.07],[297.07,297.6],[297.6,297.83],[297.83,298.29],[298.29,298.82],[298.82,299.65],[299.65,299.88],[299.88,300.18]]},{"el":"s1","text":"The link to today is direct.","start":301.06,"end":302.89,"words":[[301.11,301.23],[301.23,301.47],[301.47,301.59],[301.59,301.9],[301.9,302.02],[302.02,302.5]]},{"el":"s1","text":"Many of these attacks started with one false accusation to the authorities, which is the harm the CAREN Act targets.","start":303.24,"end":310.35,"words":[[303.29,303.6],[303.6,303.76],[303.76,303.99],[303.99,304.38],[304.38,304.84],[304.84,305.07],[305.07,305.31],[305.31,305.62],[305.62,306.39],[306.39,306.54],[306.54,306.7],[306.7,307.39],[307.67,307.98],[307.98,308.13],[308.13,308.28],[308.28,308.52],[308.52,308.67],[308.67,309.06],[309.06,309.29],[309.29,309.83]]},{"el":"h2","text":"Driven out and taken","start":311.25,"end":312.93,"words":[[311.29,311.79],[311.79,312.04],[312.04,312.29],[312.29,312.8]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Forsyth County, Georgia, 1912: after a lynching, night riders drove out about 1,100 Black residents.","start":313.63,"end":321.95,"words":[[313.65,313.98],[313.98,314.45],[314.68,315.15],[315.38,316.24],[316.47,316.74],[316.74,316.87],[316.87,317.4],[317.63,317.9],[317.9,318.3],[318.3,318.63],[318.63,318.83],[318.83,319.16],[319.16,320.48],[320.48,320.75],[320.75,321.41]]},{"el":"s2","text":"White neighbors took over their farms.","start":322.3,"end":324.49,"words":[[322.35,322.61],[322.61,323.0],[323.0,323.19],[323.19,323.45],[323.45,323.65],[323.65,323.98]]},{"el":"s2","text":"The county stayed almost entirely white for 75 years.","start":324.84,"end":328.78,"words":[[324.9,325.03],[325.03,325.43],[325.43,325.77],[325.77,326.23],[326.23,326.76],[326.76,327.03],[327.03,327.16],[327.16,327.9],[327.9,328.23]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Sundown towns: historian James Loewen documented hundreds, possibly thousands, of towns that kept Black people out by violence or law, often after driving out those already there (see the old laws tracker).","start":329.13,"end":342.81,"words":[[329.14,329.67],[329.67,330.12],[330.39,331.07],[331.07,331.53],[331.53,331.98],[331.98,332.82],[332.82,333.5],[333.77,334.3],[334.3,334.98],[335.25,335.4],[335.4,335.78],[335.78,336.01],[336.01,336.31],[336.31,336.62],[336.62,337.0],[337.0,337.22],[337.22,337.45],[337.45,338.06],[338.06,338.14],[338.14,338.36],[338.63,338.93],[338.93,339.24],[339.24,339.77],[339.77,340.0],[340.0,340.3],[340.3,340.76],[340.76,340.98],[340.98,341.14],[341.14,341.29],[341.29,341.59],[341.59,341.82],[341.82,342.28]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Seneca Village, New York, 1857: the city's largest community of Black property owners was taken by eminent domain and razed to build Central Park.","start":343.16,"end":353.58,"words":[[343.22,343.66],[343.66,344.16],[344.42,344.63],[344.63,344.92],[345.17,346.33],[346.58,346.73],[346.73,347.09],[347.09,347.59],[347.59,348.24],[348.24,348.39],[348.39,348.67],[348.67,349.18],[349.18,349.54],[349.54,349.76],[349.76,350.19],[350.19,350.41],[350.41,350.91],[350.91,351.35],[351.35,351.56],[351.56,351.92],[351.92,352.07],[352.07,352.36],[352.36,352.86],[352.86,353.15]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Bruce's Beach, California, 1924: a Black-owned beach resort was seized by the city of Manhattan Beach.","start":353.93,"end":361.18,"words":[[353.99,354.4],[354.4,354.75],[354.99,355.69],[355.93,357.04],[357.28,357.42],[357.42,357.97],[357.97,358.25],[358.25,358.6],[358.6,358.81],[358.81,359.08],[359.08,359.29],[359.29,359.43],[359.43,359.71],[359.71,359.84],[359.84,360.4],[360.4,360.75]]},{"el":"s2","text":"In 2022 Los Angeles County returned it to the Bruce family's descendants, an early example of returning stolen land.","start":361.53,"end":369.64,"words":[[361.58,361.73],[361.73,362.75],[362.75,362.96],[362.96,363.54],[363.54,363.98],[363.98,364.41],[364.41,364.56],[364.56,364.7],[364.7,364.85],[364.85,365.14],[365.14,365.65],[365.65,366.44],[366.7,366.84],[366.84,367.06],[367.06,367.64],[367.64,367.79],[367.79,368.29],[368.29,368.8],[368.8,369.16]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Chavez Ravine, Los Angeles, 1950s: Mexican American neighborhoods were cleared for public housing that was never built; the land became Dodger Stadium.","start":369.99,"end":381.01,"words":[[370.04,370.49],[370.49,370.94],[371.21,371.43],[371.43,372.11],[372.37,373.43],[373.69,374.29],[374.29,374.9],[374.9,375.57],[375.57,375.72],[375.72,376.1],[376.1,376.25],[376.25,376.7],[376.7,377.16],[377.16,377.38],[377.38,377.61],[377.61,377.91],[377.91,378.29],[378.55,378.7],[378.7,379.0],[379.0,379.45],[379.45,379.83],[379.83,380.51]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Black farmland: around 1910, Black farmers owned roughly 15 million acres.","start":381.36,"end":387.03,"words":[[381.41,381.69],[381.69,382.26],[382.51,382.94],[382.94,383.72],[383.97,384.26],[384.26,384.61],[384.61,384.9],[384.9,385.25],[385.25,385.68],[385.68,386.11],[386.11,386.54]]},{"el":"s2","text":"Most of it is gone.","start":387.38,"end":389.14,"words":[[387.45,387.85],[387.85,388.01],[388.01,388.17],[388.17,388.33],[388.33,388.64]]},{"el":"s2","text":"It was lost to violence, to the U.S. Department of Agriculture's discrimination in loans (settled in Pigford v. Glickman, 1999), and to forced sales of “heirs' property” passed down without a will.","start":389.49,"end":402.68,"words":[[389.54,389.68],[389.68,389.89],[389.89,390.17],[390.17,390.31],[390.31,390.93],[391.18,391.32],[391.32,391.46],[391.46,391.81],[392.16,392.79],[392.79,392.93],[392.93,393.69],[393.69,394.67],[394.67,394.81],[394.81,395.16],[395.16,395.58],[395.58,395.72],[395.72,396.14],[396.14,396.35],[396.7,397.26],[397.5,398.76],[399.0,399.21],[399.21,399.35],[399.35,399.63],[399.63,399.98],[399.98,400.12],[400.12,400.33],[400.33,400.82],[400.82,401.1],[401.1,401.38],[401.38,401.8],[401.8,401.94],[401.94,402.22]]},{"el":"h3","text":"Flooded","start":403.58,"end":404.3,"words":[[403.58,404.18]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Oscarville, Georgia: its Black residents were driven out in 1912.","start":405.0,"end":409.98,"words":[[405.06,405.61],[405.85,406.33],[406.58,406.78],[406.78,407.06],[407.06,407.68],[407.68,407.82],[407.82,408.23],[408.23,408.44],[408.44,408.58],[408.58,409.48]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The land was later flooded to create Lake Lanier (Buford Dam, 1956).","start":410.33,"end":416.03,"words":[[410.37,410.51],[410.51,410.8],[410.8,411.02],[411.02,411.39],[411.39,411.83],[411.83,411.97],[411.97,412.49],[412.49,412.78],[412.78,413.29],[413.29,413.73],[413.73,414.02],[414.28,415.52]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Great Mississippi Flood, 1927: Black residents were held in levee camps under armed guard and forced to work on the levees, while white residents were evacuated.","start":416.38,"end":427.01,"words":[[416.43,416.57],[416.57,416.92],[416.92,417.49],[417.49,417.85],[418.1,419.45],[419.7,419.98],[419.98,420.62],[420.62,420.76],[420.76,421.05],[421.05,421.19],[421.19,421.47],[421.47,421.83],[421.83,422.11],[422.11,422.33],[422.33,422.54],[422.54,422.75],[422.75,423.04],[423.04,423.18],[423.18,423.39],[423.39,423.54],[423.54,423.68],[423.68,424.1],[424.35,424.64],[424.64,424.92],[424.92,425.56],[425.56,425.7],[425.7,426.56]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Vanport, Oregon, 1948: Oregon's largest Black community, built for wartime shipyard workers, was destroyed when a dike broke.","start":427.36,"end":437.29,"words":[[427.41,427.93],[428.2,428.72],[428.98,430.18],[430.44,430.96],[430.96,431.49],[431.49,431.79],[431.79,432.53],[432.79,433.09],[433.09,433.24],[433.24,433.69],[433.69,434.14],[434.14,434.59],[434.85,435.08],[435.08,435.67],[435.67,435.9],[435.9,436.05],[436.05,436.35],[436.35,436.8]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Survivors were pushed into Portland's Albina neighborhood, which was later cut up by a freeway and then gentrified.","start":437.64,"end":444.06,"words":[[437.66,438.19],[438.19,438.32],[438.32,438.59],[438.59,438.85],[438.85,439.38],[439.38,439.78],[439.78,440.37],[440.61,440.87],[440.87,441.07],[441.07,441.4],[441.4,441.6],[441.6,441.73],[441.73,441.93],[441.93,442.06],[442.06,442.46],[442.46,442.66],[442.66,442.86],[442.86,443.65]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Lower Ninth Ward, New Orleans, 2005: levee failures during Hurricane Katrina flooded this mostly Black neighborhood.","start":444.41,"end":453.13,"words":[[444.46,444.61],[444.61,444.9],[444.9,445.27],[445.27,445.56],[445.82,446.04],[446.04,446.55],[446.81,447.84],[448.1,448.39],[448.39,448.91],[448.91,449.42],[449.42,450.01],[450.01,450.53],[450.53,450.97],[450.97,451.19],[451.19,451.7],[451.7,452.0],[452.0,452.66]]},{"el":"s3","text":"The Road Home rebuilding program based grants on pre-storm home values, so Black homeowners in undervalued neighborhoods got less; the state and HUD settled a lawsuit in 2011.","start":453.48,"end":465.47,"words":[[453.53,453.68],[453.68,453.99],[453.99,454.29],[454.29,454.9],[454.9,455.51],[455.51,455.89],[455.89,456.35],[456.35,456.5],[456.5,457.04],[457.04,457.34],[457.34,457.88],[458.15,458.37],[458.37,458.68],[458.68,459.37],[459.37,459.52],[459.52,460.28],[460.28,460.97],[460.97,461.2],[461.2,461.51],[461.77,461.92],[461.92,462.31],[462.31,462.54],[462.54,462.76],[462.76,463.22],[463.22,463.38],[463.38,463.76],[463.76,463.91],[463.91,464.98]]},{"el":"s3","text":"Much of the neighborhood was never rebuilt.","start":465.82,"end":468.05,"words":[[465.87,466.1],[466.1,466.22],[466.22,466.34],[466.34,466.8],[466.8,466.98],[466.98,467.21],[467.21,467.61]]},{"el":"h4","text":"Bulldozed: urban renewal and highways","start":468.95,"end":471.64,"words":[[469.0,469.66],[469.92,470.22],[470.22,470.74],[470.74,470.96],[470.96,471.47]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The Housing Act of 1949 and the Federal-Aid Highway Act of 1956 sent billions of federal dollars to clear “blight,” a label usually given to the same neighborhoods the redlining maps had marked red.","start":472.34,"end":486.33,"words":[[472.39,472.55],[472.55,473.02],[473.02,473.26],[473.26,473.41],[473.41,474.59],[474.59,474.83],[474.83,474.99],[474.99,475.77],[475.77,476.24],[476.24,476.48],[476.48,476.64],[476.64,477.89],[477.89,478.21],[478.21,478.76],[478.76,478.91],[478.91,479.46],[479.46,479.86],[479.86,480.01],[480.01,480.33],[480.33,480.8],[480.8,480.96],[480.96,481.43],[481.43,481.98],[481.98,482.37],[482.37,482.53],[482.53,482.68],[482.68,483.0],[483.0,483.71],[483.71,483.86],[483.86,484.57],[484.57,484.88],[484.88,485.12],[485.12,485.43],[485.43,485.75]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Hundreds of thousands of families were displaced, people of color far out of proportion to their numbers.","start":486.68,"end":493.01,"words":[[486.72,487.3],[487.3,487.44],[487.44,488.02],[488.02,488.16],[488.16,488.67],[488.67,488.81],[488.81,489.53],[489.78,490.14],[490.14,490.29],[490.29,490.58],[490.58,490.72],[490.72,490.94],[490.94,491.08],[491.08,491.66],[491.66,491.8],[491.8,492.02],[492.02,492.52]]},{"el":"s4","text":"James Baldwin called it “Negro removal.”","start":493.36,"end":496.19,"words":[[493.36,493.79],[493.79,494.3],[494.3,494.58],[494.58,494.73],[494.73,495.16],[495.16,495.73]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The list of Black business districts and neighborhoods cut apart is long: Rondo in St. Paul (I-94), Overtown in Miami (I-95), Tremé in New Orleans (I-10 over Claiborne Avenue), Hayti in Durham, Black Bottom and Paradise Valley in Detroit (I-375), Sweet Auburn in Atlanta, the Hill District in Pittsburgh, the Fillmore in San Francisco, Albina in Portland, and Greenwood in Tulsa a second time.","start":496.54,"end":525.37,"words":[[496.6,496.76],[496.76,497.06],[497.06,497.21],[497.21,497.52],[497.52,497.97],[497.97,498.66],[498.66,498.89],[498.89,499.57],[499.57,499.8],[499.8,500.11],[500.11,500.26],[500.26,500.56],[500.83,501.29],[501.29,501.44],[501.44,501.82],[502.2,502.43],[502.43,503.27],[503.54,504.15],[504.15,504.3],[504.3,504.83],[504.83,505.82],[506.09,506.55],[506.55,506.7],[506.7,506.93],[506.93,507.38],[507.38,507.76],[507.76,508.07],[508.07,508.6],[508.6,509.14],[509.4,509.78],[509.78,509.94],[509.94,510.39],[510.66,510.96],[510.96,511.34],[511.34,511.57],[511.57,512.18],[512.18,512.49],[512.49,512.64],[512.64,513.17],[513.17,515.15],[515.42,515.73],[515.73,516.03],[516.03,516.18],[516.18,516.79],[517.06,517.21],[517.21,517.44],[517.44,518.05],[518.05,518.2],[518.2,518.81],[519.08,519.23],[519.23,519.61],[519.61,519.76],[519.76,519.99],[519.99,520.83],[521.1,521.55],[521.55,521.71],[521.71,522.32],[522.58,522.81],[522.81,523.34],[523.34,523.5],[523.5,523.88],[523.88,524.03],[524.03,524.49],[524.49,524.87]]},{"el":"s4","text":"Some of this is now being undone.","start":525.72,"end":528.43,"words":[[525.76,526.03],[526.03,526.2],[526.2,526.47],[526.47,526.65],[526.65,526.91],[526.91,527.35],[527.35,527.88]]},{"el":"s4","text":"The 2021 infrastructure law created a Reconnecting Communities program, Detroit is replacing I-375 with a boulevard, and Minnesota's transportation department apologized for Rondo in 2015.","start":528.78,"end":543.38,"words":[[528.84,528.97],[528.97,530.01],[530.01,530.91],[530.91,531.04],[531.04,531.66],[531.66,531.8],[531.8,532.49],[532.49,533.18],[533.18,533.8],[534.04,534.53],[534.53,534.66],[534.66,535.29],[535.29,537.01],[537.01,537.22],[537.22,537.36],[537.36,537.91],[538.15,538.36],[538.36,539.05],[539.05,539.94],[539.94,540.56],[540.56,541.32],[541.32,541.46],[541.46,541.87],[541.87,542.01],[542.01,542.91]]},{"el":"h5","text":"Gentrification: displacement by the market","start":544.28,"end":547.95,"words":[[544.33,545.67],[545.96,546.97],[546.97,547.22],[547.22,547.39],[547.39,547.8]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Gentrification is when higher-income, usually whiter residents and investment move into a lower-income neighborhood, raising rents and property values.","start":548.65,"end":557.73,"words":[[548.7,549.7],[549.7,549.84],[549.84,550.04],[550.04,550.77],[551.01,551.48],[551.48,551.81],[551.81,552.41],[552.41,552.61],[552.61,553.28],[553.28,553.48],[553.48,553.75],[553.75,553.89],[553.89,554.56],[554.56,555.16],[555.39,555.79],[555.79,556.13],[556.13,556.33],[556.33,556.8],[556.8,557.27]]},{"el":"s5","text":"That it happens, and that many historically Black neighborhoods have changed rapidly, is documented.","start":558.08,"end":564.56,"words":[[558.13,558.34],[558.34,558.48],[558.48,558.97],[559.22,559.43],[559.43,559.64],[559.64,559.92],[559.92,560.62],[560.62,560.9],[560.9,561.53],[561.53,561.74],[561.74,562.3],[562.3,562.87],[563.11,563.25],[563.25,564.09]]},{"el":"s5","text":"A 2019 study by the National Community Reinvestment Coalition estimated that gentrification displaced more than 135,000 Black and Hispanic residents from 2000 to 2013, concentrated in cities like Washington, Portland and Seattle.","start":564.91,"end":583.62,"words":[[564.96,565.11],[565.11,566.08],[566.08,566.46],[566.46,566.69],[566.69,566.84],[566.84,567.36],[567.36,568.04],[568.04,569.01],[569.01,569.69],[569.69,570.44],[570.44,570.67],[570.67,571.79],[571.79,572.47],[572.47,572.62],[572.62,572.84],[572.84,574.87],[574.87,575.17],[575.17,575.4],[575.4,576.0],[576.0,576.68],[576.68,576.98],[576.98,577.65],[577.65,577.8],[577.8,578.7],[578.97,579.94],[579.94,580.09],[580.09,580.47],[580.47,580.77],[580.77,581.44],[581.71,582.23],[582.23,582.46],[582.46,583.06]]},{"el":"s5","text":"What's debated is how much gentrification itself pushes people out, as opposed to normal moving.","start":583.97,"end":589.74,"words":[[584.01,584.28],[584.28,584.8],[584.8,584.93],[584.93,585.13],[585.13,585.39],[585.39,586.37],[586.37,586.77],[586.77,587.1],[587.1,587.42],[587.42,587.69],[587.91,588.05],[588.05,588.44],[588.44,588.57],[588.57,588.9],[588.9,589.29]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Some economists find that longtime residents of gentrifying areas are not much more likely to move than residents elsewhere.","start":590.09,"end":598.14,"words":[[590.13,590.36],[590.36,591.14],[591.14,591.52],[591.52,591.76],[591.76,592.3],[592.3,592.99],[592.99,593.15],[593.15,594.07],[594.07,594.54],[594.54,594.61],[594.61,594.85],[594.85,595.16],[595.16,595.31],[595.31,595.77],[595.77,595.93],[595.93,596.16],[596.16,596.39],[596.39,597.09],[597.09,597.63]]},{"el":"s5","text":"Others find that those who do leave are pushed to poorer neighborhoods, and that rising taxes, evictions and the loss of churches and businesses damage the community even for those who stay.","start":598.49,"end":609.74,"words":[[598.55,598.85],[598.85,599.23],[599.23,599.45],[599.45,599.75],[599.75,599.91],[599.91,600.06],[600.06,600.28],[600.28,600.36],[600.36,600.66],[600.66,600.81],[600.81,601.19],[601.19,601.94],[602.2,602.43],[602.43,602.66],[602.66,603.11],[603.11,603.64],[603.9,604.5],[604.5,604.73],[604.73,604.88],[604.88,605.11],[605.11,605.26],[605.26,605.78],[605.78,606.01],[606.01,606.61],[606.61,607.07],[607.07,607.22],[607.22,607.9],[607.9,608.2],[608.2,608.35],[608.35,608.65],[608.65,608.8],[608.8,609.18]]},{"el":"s5","text":"The link to the CAREN Act is well documented: as neighborhoods change, longtime residents are reported for doing what they've always done.","start":610.09,"end":617.75,"words":[[610.14,610.27],[610.27,610.54],[610.54,610.67],[610.67,610.81],[610.81,611.14],[611.14,611.34],[611.34,611.47],[611.47,611.67],[611.67,612.47],[612.71,612.84],[612.84,613.44],[613.44,613.97],[614.21,614.67],[614.67,615.27],[615.27,615.34],[615.34,615.8],[615.8,615.94],[615.94,616.2],[616.2,616.4],[616.4,616.67],[616.67,617.07],[617.07,617.34]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In 2018 a white woman called police on a Black family barbecuing at Oakland's Lake Merritt.","start":618.1,"end":623.53,"words":[[618.15,618.28],[618.28,619.04],[619.04,619.17],[619.17,619.42],[619.42,619.74],[619.74,620.0],[620.0,620.31],[620.31,620.44],[620.44,620.57],[620.57,620.82],[620.82,621.21],[621.21,621.78],[621.78,621.91],[621.91,622.41],[622.41,622.67],[622.67,623.05]]},{"el":"s5","text":"In 2019 a new resident's complaint about go-go music in Washington's Shaw neighborhood sparked #DontMuteDC, and the city made go-go its official music.","start":623.88,"end":634.14,"words":[[623.93,624.08],[624.08,625.01],[625.01,625.15],[625.15,625.36],[625.36,626.0],[626.0,626.65],[626.65,627.0],[627.0,627.43],[627.43,627.86],[627.86,628.0],[628.0,628.64],[628.64,628.79],[628.79,629.36],[629.36,629.71],[629.71,630.93],[631.18,631.39],[631.39,631.53],[631.53,631.82],[631.82,632.11],[632.11,632.53],[632.53,632.75],[632.75,633.18],[633.18,633.67]]},{"el":"s5","text":"What helps: community land trusts that keep homes affordable for good, property-tax relief for longtime owners, tenant protections, and “right to return” policies like Portland's N/NE Preference Policy (2015), which gives families pushed out of Albina priority for new affordable housing.","start":634.49,"end":653.72,"words":[[634.55,634.77],[634.77,635.23],[635.5,636.18],[636.18,636.49],[636.49,636.95],[636.95,637.17],[637.17,637.4],[637.4,637.78],[637.78,638.39],[638.39,638.54],[638.54,638.85],[639.12,639.95],[639.95,640.33],[640.33,640.49],[640.49,641.02],[641.02,641.48],[641.74,642.2],[642.2,643.04],[643.3,643.53],[643.53,643.84],[643.84,643.99],[643.99,644.37],[644.37,644.9],[644.9,645.21],[645.21,645.82],[645.82,646.43],[646.43,647.04],[647.04,647.49],[647.49,648.48],[648.75,649.05],[649.05,649.36],[649.36,649.89],[649.89,650.2],[650.2,650.42],[650.42,650.58],[650.58,651.03],[651.03,651.72],[651.72,651.87],[651.87,652.1],[652.1,652.71],[652.71,653.24]]}]}